ands are ignored. The workflow producer owns each ordered `WorkflowSnapshot.steps` definition, state-change timestamp, optional active-mode display, and optional `currentStepComplete` boolean. Hub validates the base workflow and each decoration independently but mirrors no step ids, short codes, labels, mode vocabulary, or legacy fallback; invalid decoration does not hide a valid base workflow. One pure positional marker rule serves project rows, board cards, and the action workspace: indexes before `activeIndex` are complete; the current index is complete only when `currentStepComplete === true`, otherwise active; later indexes are pending. Direct later-step invocation therefore checks earlier positions. This is a position cue rather than sequence enforcement or an execution audit. Completion does not change lane placement, and retained terminal snapshots remain visible across stale/shutdown state until a new workflow snapshot replaces them. Base workflow state may be retained in `registry.json`, but `SessionsController` merges a mode only into `RuntimeSession` from a fresh non-shutdown heartbeat with confirmed tmux presence, so modes never affect persistence or liveness.
 - Managed-session lifecycle orchestration lives in `src/app/session-lifecycle.ts`; it owns add/start/stop/restart/fork, delete, subagent cleanup, and worktree finish/discard sequencing while reusing core adapters. Normal fork and Fork and compact share the same group-selection form; the compact variant adds only the one-shot startup behavior described above. `src/app/delete-session.ts` and `src/app/worktree-session.ts` remain explicit safety entry modules for delete and Git lifecycle flows and delegate to the central module; callers must keep using those entry paths. Normal delete stops tmux, removes any owned multi-repo workspace, removes the registry row, removes the heartbeat, and keeps Pi conversation/session files. Normal delete intentionally keeps hub-owned worktree directories. Finish or discard performs a clean-state preflight, records stage-aware per-repository evidence, and after Git reloads and stops the latest exact cascade. Verified success retains the same parent as stopped and Archived with source-root cwd values and merged/discarded cleanup history; it removes obsolete children, workspaces, and heartbeats. Partial failure keeps completed stage evidence and unresolved mappings. Multi-repo worktree finish/discard handles additional repos before the primary repo so partial failures keep the primary session cwd recoverable. Tmux, workspace, and Git work that determines a lifecycle mutation stays outside the lock and completes before the short registry update; after the registry commit, remove each session's heartbeat. `removeSessions(sessions, path, env)` filters only prepared IDs from the latest registry after tmux/workspace cleanup. Successful deletion removes only the prepared IDs from latest state, while partial worktree failure transforms the latest surviving parent after failed external work. Archive pruning is separate and dashboard-only after seven days: `src/app/controller.ts` removes expired archived registry rows plus heartbeat and owned multi-repo workspace state only after every row in the session/subagent cascade is confirmed missing from tmux. Refresh observes one tmux session-list snapshot for its initial registry and applies an observation only to a latest row with the same session ID, tmux target, and unchanged `updatedAt` version before projecting runtime fields or pruning. The TUI still pauses its local refresh loop around deletion/finish/discard for lifecycle ordering, while latest-state registry transformations independently preserve concurrent CLI, TUI, and external subagent writes.
 - Groups are implicit flat labels on sessions, not separate records. Creating a session with a new group label creates the group; pressing `g` in the TUI moves the selected session to an existing or new group, while `G` renames the selected session's current group for all sessions in that group. In Status view, pressing `K`/`J` or Shift-Up/Shift-Down reorders the selected Active or Backlog session within its current group; Archived is globally chronological and cannot be manually reordered. Pressing `A`, `B`, or `U` archives, backlogs, or restores a parent session by updating optional `ManagedSession.bucket` / `bucketChangedAt` fields; these actions never stop tmux/Pi, and subagent rows inherit their top-level parent's effective lifecycle instead of moving directly. Archived shows the five newest parent cascades by default and exposes older cascades through an ephemeral disclosure target. In the project cockpit, Backlog is row metadata rather than a section header. `NEEDS YOU` is always expanded; HEALTH, ACTIVE, QUIET, and ARCHIVED are synthetic selectable tier targets with independent persisted collapse state. Archived also retains its separate older-cascade disclosure. `Enter` and double-click toggle the selected collapsible tier, while filtering initially reveals matching rows. Tier and tree controls remain active under a filter, using temporary disclosure state that resets when the filter changes or clears; unfiltered disclosure preferences remain unchanged. The Archived header is a non-session target, so session actions remain inert; collapsing it repairs a hidden session selection to the header. Pressing `R` uses the cursor-aware form shared with in-session `Alt+R`, then writes a short-lived `name-commands/<session-id>.json` request for the exact live Pi conversation. The extension polls it alongside theme commands and calls `pi.setSessionName()` without touching editor text or submitting input. Requests expire after five seconds; wrong-conversation, pre-start, and already-applied requests are ignored. Validated generic `context.ticket` blocks Rename in the catalog, caller, and extension; blocked requests must not replay after unlinking. The producer owns ticket-title enforcement for native and agent naming. Do not infer this restriction from workflow position or copy ticket titles into Hub state. Rename succeeds only after heartbeat name confirmation, and session deletion or archive pruning removes the request file. Stopped/error rows require restart first. Normal name propagation updates the Hub cache. `N` remains manual recovery from the latest persisted Pi `session_info` name. In-session `Alt+R` keeps the explicit dashboard rename dialog because that flow temporarily leaves the managed session before switching back. Pressing `p` opens the shared send form bound to the exact Pi conversation and interaction instance. The existing interaction socket dispatches `send-message` writes through Pi's `sendUserMessage()` API with deduplicated receipts; it never pastes into the Pi editor or maintains a Hub queue. The selected interaction state supplies idle/busy delivery choices independently of dashboard runtime status. Idle Send expands commands/templates; explicit busy Steer/Queue sends literal text so slash commands cannot bypass the chosen delivery. See [Send, steer, or queue](FEATURES.md#send-steer-or-queue) for controls and reload requirements. At 120+ columns, pressing `Enter` on a stopped or error row restarts it instead of attempting to attach. Below 120, `i` opens the exact selected action workspace without side effects; `Enter` remains the direct open/switch/restart action at every width. Pressing `r` opens restart choices: `r` resumes the selected saved Pi conversation, `n` clears the selected hub row's Pi session metadata and starts a new Pi conversation in the same tmux/session record, and `a` restarts all active non-subagent sessions while skipping Backlog and Archived. Do not add separate empty-group lifecycle unless the model changes.
 - Explicit session closure is Hub-owned registry state (`ManagedSession.closure`), independent of producer workflow completion and runtime liveness. `src/core/session-bucket.ts` owns close/restore transformations; `src/app/controller.ts` applies them to the latest parent/cascade state. The `A` catalog entry opens the `SessionDialog` Archive chooser: `d` Done and `x` Abandoned use `closeSession`, while `a` Archive only uses the bucket move and never infers an outcome. All three share one `archiveSession(id, closure?, expected)` callback and `archiveDashboardSession()` pin cleanup in `src/app/run-tui.ts`; neither stops Pi. The chooser binds the parent and available Pi conversation identity, rechecks it before exact pin detach and again at the latest-state registry mutation, and consumes every key so `d`/`x`/`a` never reach Delete, close-pin or Mark read. `A` and `B` are unavailable while closed; `U` is the only Reopen and clears closure. Fresh-conversation restart clears closure without changing the bucket, while resume and heartbeat refresh preserve it. No Rules or heartbeat completion flag is involved. User controls and marker meanings live in [Session closure](FEATURES.md#session-closure).
 - Active and Backlog source rows retain stable `default`-first group order. Inside each group, errors come first, then unacknowledged `waiting` rows newest-first, `starting`/`running`, acknowledged `waiting`/`idle` rows by activity, and stopped rows. The project cockpit then stable-partitions complete owner trees into its presentation tiers without changing that source order inside a tier; each Active fleet parent carries its chosen group badge once below the title; single-line lifecycle and board rows retain the badge before the title. `ManagedSession.order` is optional registry data, breaks exact priority/activity ties, and falls back to group-relative registry order; new/forked/group-moved sessions append to the target group within the current lifecycle section. `K`/`J` reorder only inside the selected row's current tie. `src/core/session-order.ts` owns this ordering while keeping Archived globally newest-first by archive time; every nonempty cockpit tier renders its heading. `src/core/session-tree.ts` builds on `src/core/session-order.ts` and owns both recursive subagent nesting and canonical ancestry lookup for navigation and render projections. Build one `createSessionTreeIndex()` per behaviorally meaningful row scope and reuse its cached trace; do not merge filtered, active, projected, lane-local, or raw-snapshot scopes. The index supplies ownership, depth, and linked-parent facts, while each projection applies its own unresolved-ancestry policy: archive lifecycle falls back to the row when a parent is missing; the project cockpit keeps missing-parent and cyclic terminal rows as standalone presentation owners; board lane and expansion ownership omit ownerless subagents; and archive lifecycle plus board grouping use the terminal cycle member. Titles never affect order, and stopped sessions remain in the group instead of rendering in a separate subsection.
 - The selected-session action workspace uses only validated producer context, current runtime projections, workflow/status evidence, and catalog command descriptors. For a supported pending questionnaire, a separate **Answer** command becomes primary and opens the inline answer area in Conversation; `open` becomes **Open in Pi** and focuses the real questionnaire through exact pin focus or normal attach. The workspace does not mirror questionnaire options; direct answers go through the producer's exact pending resolver. A handoff's optional recommendation and artifact render as rows in the request block. Hub never reads the artifact, and `copy-path` encodes the request ID in its command ID, so the exact-ID guard rejects stale clicks. Hub does not capture, store, render, or search tmux pane tails, and it never scans Pi conversation files; Conversation renders only typed content from the live interaction bridge. `src/app/refresh-loop.ts` refreshes observation state only; `SessionsController` has no preview snapshot or capture invalidation path.
