gether. Descendant activity and modes stay out of the archived row tail. Lifecycle still controls row/card richness and glyph treatment. In stage grouping, lane headers and producer workflow-tree projection apply independently of project card richness. Dashboard rendering must be safe at any pane size: `SessionsView.render()` owns the below-40-col narrow notice, `renderSessions()` bounds output to terminal height, and mouse hit maps must describe only visible rows. The stage-grouping projection reuses `RenderSection`: it chooses one deterministic producer pipeline from filtered Active parents, keeps compatible trees in producer lane order, then appends synthetic `OTHER ACTIVE` for every workflowless or incompatible Active parent tree. Backlog/Archived stay footer-only. Every section nests rows under the top-level parent's existing group; headings count parents only, each parent carries one group badge before the title, and lower metadata does not repeat it. Subagent descendants are omitted by default through separate ephemeral project/stage `SessionsView` expansion sets. `←`/`→` collapses or expands the selected top-level tree; `Shift+←`/`Shift+→` applies to every tree in the current grouping; `Space` remains a stage-group selected-tree toggle. `▸`/`▾` reflects projection state, collapsing a selected descendant repairs selection to its parent, and an active filter initially reveals matching tree context without mutating either set. Selected-tree and all-tree controls then use filter-local folds, independently for fleet and board. Only visible children receive row targets. Optional producer modes substitute active-step display without entering pipeline identity or lane selection; stopped rows suppress mode decoration while retaining their base lane. Top-level parent rows derive `⚙︎N` from descendant subagents currently `starting` or `running`; the count is transient, omitted at zero, and never promotes child runtime or attention. For waiting/idle rows in both grouping modes, normalized producer attention uses `✓`, `?`, or `!`; running/error/stopped presentation remains authoritative, and attention is projected per row without parent promotion. Active parent rows can add bounded request or ticket context and ticket metadata. Backlog and Archived parents remain single-line. Subagents use micro rows with capped visual depth, agent name, task preview, and their independent status/attention; they omit parent rails, age, workflow, and metadata adornments. Pin mode uses the same title/metadata pair for Active parents, without task continuation lines; the bottom decision strip retains selected-session context and actions. On the workflow board at 100+ columns, producer activity and valid plan counts compose one recap with an eight-cell `■`/`□` bar and exact completed/total count; activity truncates before the reserved bar. Narrow boards suppress group headings and continuation lines. Active fleet parents place the session's chosen `[group]` badge and dim `⧉ N` count in the lower metadata line. Board and single-line lifecycle rows keep the badge before the title and count after it. Badges use dim brackets and primary-text contents; the worktree `⎇` remains immediately before the title. Children retain compact micro rows. The action workspace retains the actual repository, full branch and compact repository count. The optional mode short and `⚙︎N` live below Active fleet titles; board and single-line rows retain their width-dependent adornments. Fleet top chrome reserves the full `FLEET`, `WORKFLOW`, or `PINNED FLEET` label before fitting right-aligned owner-tree, pin, explicit-attention, and health signals. The filter bar above results owns editing/applied state and uses the projection's matching/total owner counts, not folded or windowed row counts; it has no action target. Full-width workspace rendering starts with selected-session identity instead of a second mode header; filtered project and board counts always show visible/total owner trees. At 100+ columns, normal Status view renders a separate five-tier navigator: 16 cells at 100–119 and 17 cells at 120+. Its `TIERS` heading is distinct from the `FLEET` mode label, and its owner counts come from an independent projection that retains zero tiers and supports standalone missing-parent/cyclic presentation owners. Navigator rows are mouse-only and resolve a fresh exact owner destination; they never enter keyboard order or command targeting. Navigator, fleet, separators, workspace, and outer border use disjoint x-aware hit regions. The navigator is hidden in Repo view, board, pin, narrow, and full-screen workspace modes. Height windowing keeps the exact selected title when a list row fits. When only one body row fits in filtered results, the filter state and exit cue take priority. Active fleet title/metadata pairs stay together, including in pinned panels, so progress remains scannable across every visible session rather than only the selection. If only one list row fits, it shows the selected title; with more space, its metadata takes priority over optional task text. Nearby cards retain their owning section/group context. A retained continuation carries a mouse-only owner target and never appears without its title. Internal line metadata carries owner and source tree-end identity through windowing. Normal 100+ column project and board trees receive post-window `│`/`└` gutters only when at least two rich lines survive; a clipped tree cannot claim a false ending, and `NEEDS YOU` alone uses the warning tone. Every surviving line from the selected owner tree receives `selectedBg`, while `▌`, navigation, commands, and row targets retain the exact selected session. At 120–159 columns the action workspace reserves 34 cells; at 160+ it reserves 44 cells. It renders positive-only conditional content in this order: selected identity, optional closure outcome, explicit request, real task text, plain workflow position, exceptional Hub guidance, enabled catalog actions, and optional appended evidence. Task text uses the complete ticket subtitle when present and falls back to the description instead of concatenating content that can end as a dangling fragment. Missing categories contribute no rows, the first policy-ordered command carries the primary marker, and selected-subagent task/owner context stays in normal task metadata rather than a separate tree block. Every actionable row, including the pinned decision strip, presents one action label bound to one exact catalog command target. At 40–119 columns `i` opens the same renderer full-width without another mode header; `Enter` and session-row double-click open the selected session directly. Short-height compaction preserves identity, explicit closure when space permits, the primary action, and requested evidence without leaving hidden hit targets. The Archived disclosure remains a synthetic non-session target. Selected-owner backgrounds and tree gutters apply only after width fitting and windowing; footer strings stay plain in the render model for `layout.ts` to style. At sidebar width, show labeled primary controls plus `? Help`. Pressing `t` opens the dedicated theme dialog over built-in and global Pi theme resources. Preview updates `SessionsView`, dashboard status chrome, and sidebar borders only; `Escape` restores and `Enter` persists. Pi global settings are canonical while sync is enabled by default; detached mode persists `dashboard.theme`, and session entry never changes dashboard colors. Automatic appearance is read at startup and through the existing theme refresh loop, using macOS system appearance or the process's `COLORFGBG` on other platforms. Keep appearance refresh separate from the one-time Pi sync command; see [Theme behavior](CONFIG.md#theme-behavior). Tmux chrome uses the same `SessionsTheme` structurally while keeping writes outside pure render code. Contextual capability hints stay quiet and data-backed.
